{
  "abstract": "Can feedback on different training questions teach a small mathematical policy which verified support updates are useful? We adapt established learning-to-reweight methods to verifier-only, full-weight Qwen3-0.6B-Base RLVR, and separate finite optimizer influence from native-policy improvement. A calibrated analysis distinguishes clipping invariance, fresh-Adam sign invariance and responsive gradient mixtures. Deliberately regrouped recorded gradients change an executed step by 0.861 of the uniform-step norm, demonstrating available control without establishing useful learning. In the frozen native comparison, the learned controller scores 222/512, versus unselected Warm 231, uniform RLOO 264 and an ordinary nucleus/std/EOS package 240 at equal exposure. The controller's advancement rule fails. Uniform's exploratory +33/512 lead motivates a separately frozen confirmation with new ancestry and training questions. On the reserved700 primary panel, uniform scores 318 versus Warm 295 and ordinary 301, but its paired 95% intervals are [0,6.57] and [−0.86,5.71] percentage points, failing the original strictly-positive-lower-bound conjunction. On the reused512 secondary panel, the counts are 246, 256 and 259. Confirmation work coverage passes; the earlier ordinary work control retains a 42.4-second shortfall when later finite-study costs are added. We preserve failed attempts, allocation uncertainty, compact candidate evidence and executable checks. These two realized training comparisons reject promoting this controller or treating the exploratory uniform lead as confirmed. They do not isolate components, establish equivalence, or bound a method family or model capacity.",
